  "updates": [
    {
      "passage_id": "8d07582c-fb4f-468d-a0b7-c13b0508f3ba",
      "slug": "completion-how-a-camera-works",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "A camera, whether the one in a pocket phone or an expensive professional device, solves\nthe same basic problem: how to capture the light reflecting off a scene and turn it into\na still picture. In this respect it works much like the human eye, and its parts do\nroughly similar jobs.\n\nLight begins its journey when it bounces off everything in the frame. It first passes\nthrough the lens, a curved piece of glass or plastic that bends and focuses the rays so\nthat they meet neatly on the other side. Without this focusing, the image would be a\nblur. Just behind the lens sits the aperture, an adjustable opening through which the\nlight must pass. By widening or narrowing this hole, the photographer controls how much\nlight is let in.\n\nNext comes the shutter. This is a barrier that opens for a set length of time and then\ncloses again, deciding how long light is allowed in. A long opening time brightens dark\nscenes, but it also blurs anything that is moving; that is why fast-moving subjects need\na brief shutter to keep them sharp. Photographers therefore balance the aperture and the\nshutter together, trading a wider opening against a shorter exposure to suit the scene.\n\nFinally, the light reaches the recording part. In older cameras this was film, a strip\ncoated with light-sensitive chemicals that changed when exposed. Most modern cameras use\na digital sensor instead, a chip covered in millions of tiny dots that convert light\ninto electrical signals. These signals are then processed and stored as a file we view\non a screen.\n\nUnderstanding how these four parts — lens, aperture, shutter and sensor — work together\nshows that photography is less magical than it appears, although balancing them\nperfectly can still take a lifetime to master.",
      "expected_metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Văn quy trình dày bị động lược tác nhân — khi câu hỏi hỏi 'cái gì được làm với X', tìm cấu trúc 'X is/are + V3' để định vị bước cần điền.",
            "point": "Câu bị động lược tác nhân trong văn mô tả kỹ thuật",
            "review": "Bị động: S + be + V3. Khi liệt kê nhiều hành động cùng chủ ngữ, có thể dùng chung trợ động từ be: are processed and stored.",
            "example": "These signals **are then processed and stored** as a file",
            "analysis": "Hai bị động hiện tại đơn 'are processed' và 'are stored' (lược 'are' ở vế sau) cùng chia một trợ động từ, mô tả các bước nối tiếp. Tác nhân (mạch điện/phần mềm) bị lược vì trọng tâm là điều XẢY RA với tín hiệu, không phải ai thực hiện."
          },
          {
