      "slug": "completion-how-honey-is-made",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "A single honey bee may make only about one twelfth of a teaspoon of honey in its whole\nlife, yet together a hive can produce many kilograms of it each year. The story of how\nhoney is made is one of nature's cleverest examples of teamwork.\n\nEverything begins with flowers. A worker bee flies from bloom to bloom and drinks a\nsweet liquid called nectar. It keeps this liquid in a separate organ called the honey\nstomach, apart from the stomach it uses for its own food. When it is full, the bee flies\nback to the hive.\n\nBack at the hive, the real work starts. The nectar is passed from one bee to another by\nmouth, and each time it is passed on, special substances are added that help to change\nit. The nectar is still very thin at this stage, so the bees fan it with their wings to\ndrive off the water. Slowly, the water is removed and the thin nectar turns into a\nthick, sticky syrup — this is honey.\n\nFinally, the bees pump the honey into small six-sided cells made of wax. Once a cell is\nfull, they seal it with a thin wax lid. Sealed in this way, the honey can be kept for\nmany months and will feed the whole hive through the cold winter, when no flowers are\nleft to visit.",
      "expected_metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Bài process/flowchart hầu như luôn dùng bị động; bám động từ bị động (is passed/is removed) để xác định đúng bước cần điền.",
            "point": "Bị động chỉ các bước trong quy trình (be + V3)",
            "review": "Bị động: S + be + V3. Khi tả quy trình, người/vật thực hiện thường bị lược bỏ để nhấn vào hành động và đối tượng.",
