      "slug": "detail-the-truth-about-owls",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "Few birds are as misunderstood as the owl. In many stories it stands for wisdom or bad\nluck, yet the truth about its biology is stranger than any legend. There are around 250\nspecies of owl, living almost everywhere in the world except the iciest regions, and\nthey are all sharp-eyed hunters.\n\nOne of the owl's best-known features is its flexible neck. An owl can turn its head\nabout three-quarters of a full circle, far more than a person can. It needs this skill\nbecause its tube-shaped eyes are fixed in place and cannot move. To look to the side, an\nowl must swing its whole head rather than shift its eyes.\n\nOwls are also remarkably quiet fliers. The edges of their feathers are soft and shaped\nlike a comb, breaking up the sound of the air passing over them, so the owl can fly in\nalmost complete silence. This makes it nearly impossible for prey to hear it coming.\n\nMost owls are active at night and hunt after dark, relying on extremely sharp hearing to\nlocate small animals in the gloom. They swallow their prey whole, then later bring up\nthe undigested bones and fur as a small lump called a pellet. By pulling these pellets\napart, scientists can learn exactly what an owl has eaten — an unexpected window into\nthe life of a bird that few people ever see hunting.",
