    {
      "passage_id": "147e5ec0-a8a4-4610-ad6e-5c287748fddf",
      "slug": "how-bridges-stay-up",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "A heavy lorry rolls across a long bridge, yet the bridge does not fall. How does it\ncarry such an **enormous** weight? The answer lies in two invisible **force**s that\nevery bridge must balance.\n\nThe simplest design is the **beam** bridge, a flat road resting on strong supports\ncalled **pier**s. When a truck sits in the middle, the top of the beam is squeezed\ntogether, while the bottom is stretched apart. Engineers call this squeezing\n**compression** and this stretching **tension**. A short beam handles both forces\neasily, but a long one will **sag** and crack.\n\nFor very wide gaps, builders choose the suspension bridge. Tall towers hold huge steel\n**cable**s, and the cables carry the road below. The weight of traffic pulls on the\ncables in tension, and the cables pass that **load** down to the towers and deep\n**foundation**s. This clever path lets a suspension bridge span more than a kilometre.\n\nWind, heat, and earthquakes also push on every bridge. Designers add small gaps and\n**flexible** joints so the **structure** can move a little without breaking. A good\nbridge is never truly still; it bends, **sway**s, and adjusts all day. The safest\nbridges may be the ones that are quietly allowed to move.",
