    {
      "passage_id": "890b3ebf-bb92-4ab4-9e89-81225eb1b0cc",
      "slug": "inference-the-ethics-of-algorithms",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "There was a time when deciding who received a loan, who was shortlisted for an interview, or who drew the attention of the police was the work of human beings who could be questioned about their judgement. Increasingly such choices are handed to algorithms — computational procedures that sort people according to patterns extracted from data — and that transfer has raised a question institutions rarely had to face before: whether a rule encoded in software is fairer, or merely harder to interrogate, than a human it replaces.\n\nThe first worry concerns bias. A model learns not from abstract principles but from records of what happened earlier, and if those records reflect past inequality, the system tends to carry the same patterns forward. What makes this especially difficult to grasp is that no discriminatory intention is required: a procedure designed to predict neutrally can still reproduce historical injustices, simply because it treats the past as a reliable guide to the future.\n\nA second difficulty concerns transparency. Many argue, reasonably, that those affected by an automated decision deserve to know how it was reached; yet even when source code is disclosed, the most sophisticated models may remain unreadable to their own creators, opaque in ways that resist plain explanation. The wish for an intelligible justification also collides with the wish to protect commercially valuable proprietary systems, and balancing the two is seldom a matter of simple rules.\n\nIt would be comforting to suppose that these problems can be programmed away, that a sufficiently advanced model will eventually outgrow the limits of the data that feeds it. The harder truth is that speed and scale do not settle questions of judgement; they merely let the consequences of a poor judgement spread faster. Accountability for an outcome cannot be quietly transferred to the software. It remains with the people who chose to deploy a tool, who defined what it optimises, and who decided which errors were acceptable — choices no machine can make on their behalf.",
