      "slug": "inference-the-paradox-of-automation",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "The conventional case for automation sounds irrefutable: machines do not tire, lose focus or panic, so the more of a process one hands to them, the safer and more dependable the system ought to be. Yet those who study automated systems have long noticed a pattern that resists this tidy logic, one memorably named, by the researcher Lisanne Bainbridge in 1983, the ironies of automation.\n\nThe central irony is this. By taking over the routine, repetitive parts of a job, an automated system removes the very practice that keeps an operator's skills sharp. This deskilling happens quietly: the operator, who now rarely controls things by hand, gradually loses the fluency that regular practice once sustained. Automation introduced to compensate for human imperfection can therefore make the remaining human contribution harder, not easier.\n\nThe difficulty grows sharpest exactly when it matters most. A machine handles the ordinary conditions but defers to the human when something abnormal arises — a rare fault, a combination of events its designers never anticipated. The operator is thus summoned not for the easy work but for the hardest, and is summoned at precisely the moment when a lack of recent practice leaves them least ready to meet it.\n\nA second variant of the problem concerns attention. Asking a person merely to watch a process that mostly runs well is asking for something humans do badly: maintaining unbroken vigilance over long stretches with nothing to do. The mind wanders, attention slackens, and the very anomaly the operator is stationed there to catch risks slipping past at the instant it occurs.\n\nIt would be comforting to assume that better machines will eventually render these worries obsolete. The harder truth is that more capable automation tends to deepen the paradox rather than ease it, because it reserves for the human ever rarer and ever more demanding situations. The answer therefore lies less in more or less automation than in designing the human's place within the system with care — keeping skills practised, attention sustained, and responsibility clearly assigned.",
