      "passage_id": "ceb4219a-5329-44b8-a87b-7596118683ee",
      "slug": "inference-the-science-of-persuasion",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "In the early 1980s the psychologist Robert Cialdini took an unusual approach to studying persuasion: he spent three years training as a car salesman, a fundraiser and a telemarketer to watch the methods from the inside. The patterns he found were remarkably consistent, and he distilled them into a short list of principles that, he argued, quietly shape a great many of the decisions people make every day.\n\nThe first is reciprocity. When someone does us a small favour — a free sample at a supermarket, an unexpected gift — we feel a faint but real pressure to return it, and buying the product is one easy way to settle the debt. The favour need not be large or even wanted; what matters is the sense of obligation it leaves behind, which a modest purchase conveniently relieves.\n\nA second principle is social proof. Faced with uncertainty, people look to the behaviour of others for guidance, treating a crowd as a signal that something must be worthwhile. A long queue outside a restaurant draws still more diners, not because they have tasted the food, but because the choices of strangers feel like evidence. A third, related lever is authority: a recommendation carries far more weight when it appears to come from a doctor, an expert or anyone in a position of trust.\n\nScarcity supplies a fourth pull. Things that seem rare or about to run out strike us as more valuable, which is why a shelf labelled \"only three left\" or an offer carrying a deadline can prompt a decision that calmer reflection might delay. The closing window does not change the product; it changes how reluctant we are to lose the chance to have it.\n\nWhat makes these principles effective is that they usually operate below conscious notice, nudging a choice before any reasoning begins. That, perhaps, is also where their remedy lies: a shopper who can name the lever being pulled is better placed to pause and ask whether the decision is truly their own. Whether such awareness is enough to outweigh habits this deeply rooted is, however, far from certain.",
      "expected_metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "'What … is that …' thường giới thiệu lời giải thích cốt lõi của tác giả → đối chiếu trực tiếp với câu hỏi về quan điểm/ý chính.",
            "point": "Câu chẻ giả (pseudo-cleft) với 'What … is that …'",
            "review": "Pseudo-cleft: What + S + V + is/was + (that) + mệnh đề — đẩy thông tin trọng tâm ra sau 'is'.",
            "example": "**What makes these principles effective is that** they usually operate below conscious notice",
            "analysis": "'What makes these principles effective' là mệnh đề danh ngữ làm chủ ngữ; 'is' là động từ chính; 'that they usually operate…' là mệnh đề danh ngữ làm bổ ngữ. Cấu trúc nhấn mạnh phần đứng sau 'is that'. Bên trong, 'makes … effective' là mẫu V + O + adj (make something + tính từ)."
          },
          {
            "tip": "Khi câu dài có nhiều 'that/which', xác định mỗi mệnh đề quan hệ bổ nghĩa cho danh từ nào — đây là kỹ thuật cốt lõi để không hiểu sai quan hệ trong câu phức.",
            "point": "Mệnh đề quan hệ xác định lồng nhau",
            "review": "Mệnh đề quan hệ có thể lồng nhau; mệnh đề quan hệ chỉ tân ngữ có thể lược bỏ 'that/which' (decisions [that] people make).",
            "example": "a short list of principles **that**, he argued, **quietly shape** a great many of the decisions people make every day",
            "analysis": "'that … quietly shape …' là mệnh đề quan hệ xác định bổ nghĩa cho 'principles'; cụm chen 'he argued' xen vào giữa (mệnh đề bình luận). Bên trong còn lồng mệnh đề quan hệ rút gọn 'people make' bổ nghĩa cho 'decisions' (lược 'that'). Một câu chứa hai tầng mệnh đề quan hệ."
          },
