      "source_body_markdown": "**A**  In 1885, the German engineer Karl Benz built what many regard as the first true\nmotor car, a three-wheeled vehicle driven by a small petrol engine. Few people trusted\nthe noisy machine at first. To prove it was reliable, his wife Bertha Benz drove it more\nthan 100 kilometres to visit her mother — the first long car journey in history.\n\n**B**  Decades earlier, the British engineer George Stephenson had turned the steam engine\ninto a practical means of transport. His famous locomotive, the Rocket, pulled carriages\nalong iron rails faster than any horse. He built some of the world's first public\nrailways, and is often called the 'Father of Railways'.\n\n**C**  On the water, the American Robert Fulton changed travel with steam. In 1807 his\nsteamboat, later known as the Clermont, carried fare-paying passengers up the Hudson\nRiver in New York. It was the first steamboat to earn a steady profit, proving that\nboats no longer needed wind or muscle to move.\n\n**D**  The bicycle, too, was transformed by a careful inventor. Earlier bicycles had one\nhuge front wheel and were dangerous to ride. In 1885 the Englishman John Kemp Starley\nbuilt the 'safety bicycle', the Rover, with two equal wheels and a chain. Far safer and\neasier to balance, it is the direct ancestor of the bicycle we ride today.",
