      "source_body_markdown": "**A** Western painting did not evolve in a straight line but split, again and again, into\nrival movements, each convinced it had found a truer way of seeing. Four of these,\nemerging within fifty years, did much to shape what is meant by modern art.\n\n**B** By the 1850s a group of French painters led by Gustave Courbet had grown tired of\nthe grand mythological and historical scenes that the academies admired. Realism, as the\nmovement was called, turned instead to the everyday lives of ordinary people — labourers,\npeasants and the urban poor — and aimed to show them honestly, without flattery or\nromance.\n\n**C** A generation later, the Impressionists pushed in a different direction. Artists such\nas Claude Monet abandoned the studio to paint outdoors, trying to capture the shifting\neffects of natural light on water, snow and city streets with quick, visible\nbrushstrokes. The movement owed its name to mockery: a critic borrowed the title of one\nof Monet's paintings, 'Impression, Sunrise', to ridicule the unfinished look of the work\nshown in 1874.\n\n**D** Around 1907, Pablo Picasso and Georges Braque went further still, dismantling the\nidea that a painting should show a scene from a single fixed point. In the style that\nbecame known as Cubism, objects were broken into geometric facets and reassembled so that\nseveral viewpoints appeared at once, as if the subject were being seen from all sides\nsimultaneously.\n\n**E** The final break came after the First World War. Inspired by new theories of the\nmind, the Surrealists — among them Salvador Dalí and René Magritte — abandoned the visible\nworld almost entirely. Following a manifesto published in 1924, they filled their canvases\nwith dreamlike, impossible images drawn from the hidden workings of the unconscious mind.\n\n**F** These movements often overlapped, and few artists fit neatly into a\nsingle label. Yet together they trace a steady loosening of old rules — a shift from\npainting the world as it looks towards painting it as it is felt or imagined.",
      "expected_metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Bị động lược tác nhân (ai vẽ) để tập trung vào kỹ thuật (objects … broken) — bám danh từ chịu tác động khi ghép feature với trường phái.",
            "point": "Câu bị động (passive voice) ở quá khứ với động từ song song",
            "review": "Bị động: be + V3; hai V3 nối bằng 'and' dùng chung 'be'. so that + S + V = sao cho/để mà (mục đích–kết quả).",
            "example": "objects **were broken** into geometric facets and **reassembled** so that several viewpoints appeared at once",
            "analysis": "'were broken … and reassembled' là bị động quá khứ; hai phân từ 'broken' và 'reassembled' nối song song bằng 'and' cùng dùng một trợ động từ 'were'. 'so that + mệnh đề' nêu kết quả của việc lắp ghép."
