      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "# Scanning — Mount Everest\n\nAt 8,849 metres, Mount Everest is the highest mountain above sea level on Earth. It sits between two countries: the south side is in Nepal, and the north side is in Tibet, in China. Many people dream of standing on the top.\n\nThe first climbers to reach the top did so in 1953. Their names were Edmund Hillary, from New Zealand, and Tenzing Norgay, a Sherpa guide from the mountains of Nepal. Today, hundreds of people try to climb Everest every year.\n\nBut the mountain is very dangerous. High up, there is little air to breathe, so it is hard to walk or even think. Climbers call the part above 8,000 metres the \"death zone\". The wind is strong, and the cold can freeze your skin in minutes.\n\nTo stay safe, climbers carry bottles of oxygen and wear thick clothes. They rest at small camps on the way up. Even so, Everest is not easy, and it may always test the people who try to reach its top.\n\n---\n\n*Practice tip: When you scan, you do not read every word. You move your eyes quickly to find one thing — a number, a name or a date.*",
      "expected_metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Câu hỏi 'In which year…' luôn gắn với quá khứ đơn — scan năm (1953) cạnh các động từ quá khứ như 'reached/did'.",
            "point": "Thì quá khứ đơn nêu sự kiện đã qua (mốc thời gian)",
