      "source_body_markdown": "Take a square **sheet** of paper, **fold** it a few times, and a small animal can\nappear in your hands. This is the art of origami, the Japanese art of paper folding.\nThe word joins \"ori\", meaning fold, and \"kami\", meaning paper. The core rule is\nsimple: the art uses folding alone, without cutting or gluing.\n\nFrom a single sheet, a skilled folder can create the **shape** of a bird, a flower, or\nan animal. The most famous model is the paper **crane**. In Japan, the crane has long\nbecome a **symbol** of peace and hope. Children sometimes fold a thousand of them\ntogether, a tradition linked to a wish for health and good fortune.\n\nOrigami looks easy, but it demands **patience**. Each fold must be **precise**, or the\nmodel will not hold its shape. Complex models use a long **pattern** of dozens of\nfolds, and one small mistake near the start can ruin the final result. Beginners often\nstart with simple shapes, such as a boat or a hat, before they try harder designs that\ntake many careful steps.\n\nIn recent years, even scientists have grown interested in origami. They have realised\nthat the same folding ideas can help design real **structures** in the world. Origami\nhas **inspired** new folding designs, from **solar panels** that open in space to tiny\nmedical tubes. In this way, an ancient hobby that rewards **skill** probably still has\nmuch to teach us.",
      "expected_metadata": {
