      "source_body_markdown": "In 1971, long before the smartphone, an American **economist** named Herbert Simon set out a\nwarning that now reads as strikingly prophetic. Reflecting on a world about to be flooded with data, he\nseemed to **foresee** the true scarcity of the coming information age. That scarcity, he\nproposed, would not be information itself, but rather the human capacity to attend to it.\n\nSimon's reasoning was disarmingly simple, and it has since become the founding idea of what\nscholars call the attention economy. An **abundance** of information, he argued, necessarily\nconsumes the attention of its recipients, so that a wealth of information creates a\ncorresponding poverty of attention. Where any resource grows scarce, it acquires value, and\nthe limited focus of millions of minds soon became something that businesses were eager to\n**deplete** and to **monetise**.\n\nThe platforms that dominate the modern internet were built, in large part, precisely to\n**harvest** that scarce resource. Rather than selling a product to the people who use them,\nmany free services sell the users themselves, packaging their time and attention for advertisers. To keep that supply\nflowing, each platform deploys an **algorithm** built to maximise **engagement**, selecting\nwhatever post, video or headline seems most likely to hold the eye a little longer.\n\nThis relentless pursuit of engagement carries a subtle social cost that its designers very\nrarely advertise. Because material capable of provoking **outrage** tends to spread fastest,\nthe systems quietly reward the very content most likely to inflame. A steady stream of\ninterruption, meanwhile, may gradually **erode** the sustained concentration that serious and\ncareful thought requires. Human attention, many critics now argue, has become a tradable\n**commodity**, harvested at a scale that earlier generations could scarcely have imagined.\n\nWhat ought to follow from this sober diagnosis is, however, far less obvious than the diagnosis\nitself. Some lawmakers wish to **regulate** the most addictive design features outright, while\nothers frame the whole question as a matter of personal **autonomy** and individual restraint.\nResearchers, for their part, remain notably **ambivalent** about the path ahead. Any durable\nremedy will probably have to reconcile commercial reality with a defence of the mind that no\nsingle measure, for the moment, appears able to guarantee.",
      "expected_metadata": {
