    {
      "passage_id": "f847c638-0283-4e60-b82c-0a80d26be936",
      "slug": "the-buried-city-of-pompeii",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "On an autumn day in the year 79 AD, the people of Pompeii looked up to see a dark\ncolumn of smoke rising from Mount Vesuvius. Within hours, the **eruption** of that\n**volcano** would erase their busy town from the map — and, by accident, save it\nfor nearly two thousand years.\n\nPompeii was a **thriving** Roman town near the Bay of Naples, home to thousands of\n**inhabitants** who lived among shops, baths and grand houses. When Vesuvius\nexploded, it first rained small stones of **pumice** and hot **ash** down on the\nrooftops. As the fall grew heavier, falling debris began to **bury** the streets.\nMany people tried to **flee** towards the sea, but a wave of scorching gas and\nash swept through the town, and a great number could not escape in time.\n\nWhat followed was a strange twist of history. The thick layer of ash that killed\nthe city also worked to **preserve** it. Walls, furniture, food and paintings were\nsealed beneath the ash almost exactly as they had been on that final day. The\ntown lay forgotten until the 1700s, when workers began to **excavate** the ruins\nand slowly uncovered a complete Roman streetscape.\n\nOne discovery proved especially moving. As the bodies of victims decayed, they\nleft hollow **cavity** shapes in the hardened ash. Researchers learned to pour\nliquid **plaster** into these spaces, producing casts that captured the exact\npositions of people in their last moments. Alongside them, ordinary\n**artefacts** such as coins, loaves of bread and writing tablets came to light.\n\nToday Pompeii draws millions of visitors and continues to surprise archaeologists,\nwho still uncover new rooms and objects. Although much remains buried and fragile,\nthe city offers a rare and detailed **insight** into how ordinary Romans truly\nlived — a window that few other sites can match.",
      "expected_metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Bị động + quá khứ hoàn thành hay xuất hiện trong văn kể lịch sử; bám mốc thời gian để xếp đúng trình tự sự kiện.",
            "point": "Bị động quá khứ + so sánh trạng thái với 'as they had been'",
            "review": "Bị động quá khứ: S + was/were + V3. Quá khứ hoàn thành (had + V3) chỉ việc xảy ra trước một mốc quá khứ khác. 'as' ở đây = 'y như'.",
            "example": "Walls, furniture, food and paintings **were sealed** beneath the ash almost exactly **as they had been** on that final day.",
            "analysis": "Bị động quá khứ đơn 'were sealed' (were + V3) với chủ ngữ số nhiều; tác nhân (lớp tro) được hàm ý, không cần 'by' rõ ràng. Mệnh đề 'as they had been' dùng quá khứ hoàn thành (had + V3) để chỉ trạng thái có trước thời điểm bị niêm phong — diễn tả trình tự hai mốc trong quá khứ."
