      "slug": "the-economics-of-happiness",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "For most of its long history, economics quietly treated rising **prosperity** as a\nreliable proxy for a better and more contented life. In 1974, however, the economist\nRichard Easterlin reported a genuinely puzzling **paradox**: within any single country\nat a given moment, richer people were indeed measurably happier than their poorer\nneighbours, yet as whole nations grew steadily wealthier over the decades, the average\nlevel of reported happiness did not necessarily rise along with them.\n\nThe finding swiftly became known as the Easterlin paradox, and it forced a sceptical\nprofession to take the slippery idea of subjective **well-being** seriously for the\nfirst time. Not only did **aggregate** national income climb decade after decade, but\nreported happiness tended stubbornly to **stagnate** alongside it, raising the awkward\nquestion of whether economic growth, beyond a certain point, delivered far less\nemotional benefit than almost everyone had assumed.\n\nTwo competing explanations were quickly offered. The first stressed the role of\n**relative** income: what truly pleases us, on this account, may be the experience of\nstanding higher than those around us, rather than any absolute figure on a payslip. The\nsecond appealed instead to hedonic **adaptation**: human beings grow used to a higher\nstandard of living remarkably fast, so that the initial joy of a pay rise soon quietly\nfades. Some studies even pointed to a **satiation** point for income, a threshold beyond\nwhich each extra pound or dollar added very little to a person's sense of contentment.\n\nThat tidy and consoling story was later vigorously **contested**. By drawing on far\nricher **longitudinal** datasets, the economists Betsey Stevenson and Justin Wolfers\nargued that income in fact still **correlates** with happiness even at very high levels,\nwith no clear satiation point at all. Much of the disagreement, it gradually turned out,\ncame down to **methodology** — to precisely how happiness is measured and which\ncountries and periods are chosen for comparison.\n\nThe debate is far from merely academic, since some governments now openly weigh\nwell-being in **policy** alongside conventional measures such as GDP. What seems clear\nis that income, though it plainly matters, is at best an imperfect **proxy** for a\nflourishing human life. Only when we ask what it is that we truly value does the deeper\nquestion — far more useful than whether wealth can buy happiness — begin to emerge, a\nmatter that, for now, remains very much open.",
