    {
      "passage_id": "20bf7569-a6d4-4673-974e-8f763ed5bfe3",
      "slug": "the-economics-of-scarcity-and-time",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "In 2013, the economist Sendhil Mullainathan and the psychologist Eldar Shafir advanced a\ndeceptively simple claim: that **scarcity** itself, whether of money or of time, reshapes\nhow the mind works.\n\nTheir argument begins with a familiar observation. Whatever people lack tends to\n**preoccupy** them, capturing attention so completely that little **bandwidth** remains\nfor anything else. This mental capture, they propose, imposes a **cognitive load** that\nquietly degrades judgement, memory and self-control, much as running too many programs\nslows a computer. In their field studies, the same individuals often appeared measurably\nsharper in moments of plenty than in moments of want, as though some mental capacity had\nbeen temporarily reclaimed.\n\nThe most striking consequence is what the authors call **tunnelling**. Under pressure,\npeople focus intensely on the pressing shortfall directly in front of them while\n**neglecting** concerns that fall outside the tunnel, such as long-term planning or\npreventive maintenance. A manager racing to meet a **deadline** may, for instance, ignore\nthe very routines that would prevent the next crisis. The narrowing is not stupidity but\nfocus turned costly, an adaptation that pays off in the moment yet quietly mortgages the\nfuture.\n\nCrucially, the same dynamics that govern financial hardship appear to govern time. Someone\nperpetually short of hours behaves much like someone perpetually short of cash: both\nborrow against the future, both value **slack** they do not possess, and both can fall\ninto a **scarcity trap** in which today's shortfall **compounds** into tomorrow's. The\nborrower repays with interest and the harried worker repays with haste, so that in both\ncases the shortfall deepens with almost every cycle.\n\nThis account is, by design, **counterintuitive**, for it suggests that the failures often\nattributed to character — procrastination, poor planning, impulsive spending — may instead\nbe partly the predictable product of a **depleted** mind operating under sustained\n**deprivation**, rather than evidence of some fixed personal **deficit**.\n\nWhat follows for policy is less certain than the diagnosis. If scarcity taxes attention,\nthen designing systems that preserve a little slack — gentler deadlines, simpler forms,\nsmall buffers — might **allocate** that scarce bandwidth more humanely. Were such modest\ninterventions adopted at scale, however, whether they could offset a shortage this\nstructural is something the evidence has yet to establish.",
