    {
      "passage_id": "568074c8-bad7-49d4-b079-0e7cf8b36643",
      "slug": "the-future-of-libraries",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "When the internet spread in the 1990s, many critics predicted that it would make\npublic libraries **obsolete**. Yet three decades later, library buildings are still\nfull of **patron**s — they simply arrive for very different reasons. The story of how\nlibraries survived is really a story of reinvention.\n\nFor most of its history, the library was mainly a **repository**: a place to store\nbooks and to organise their **lending**. That role still matters, and digital lending\nnow lets users borrow e-books and audiobooks without leaving home. The New York\nPublic Library, for example, offers more than 300,000 e-books and e-audiobooks for\nfree through its digital platforms. This free access\nmatters, because it helps people who cannot afford their own **subscription**s to keep\nreading, studying and staying informed.\n\nThe deeper change, however, lies elsewhere. Many branches now run **makerspace**s\nfitted with 3D printers, sewing machines and recording equipment, where members can\nbuild, experiment and learn new skills for free. Staff hold free classes in digital\n**literacy**, helping patrons use technology with confidence, and they assist people\nwith everything from job applications to government forms. **Archive**s preserve local\nhistory and rare documents, while **outreach** programmes carry books to schools,\ncare homes and distant neighbourhoods that have no branch of their own.\n\nIn this way the library has quietly become a **civic** space — a community **hub**\nwhere people meet, learn and look for help. Because it is open to everyone, it remains\none of the few **inclusive** places left that does not ask anyone to buy something or\neven to spend any money to stay. For many residents, it is the warm, safe space they\nturn to when no other is available.\n\nWhether this model can be sustained financially remains uncertain, since public\nbudgets are always under strain. Even so, as long as libraries keep widening what\nthey offer, they may stay far more **relevant** than many people once expected.",
      "expected_metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Gặp 'predicted/said that … would …', hiểu đây là dự đoán trong quá khứ — đừng nhầm với câu điều kiện; kiểm tra xem dự đoán đó cuối cùng có thành sự thật trong bài không.",
            "point": "Câu điều kiện loại 2 / dạng tường thuật với 'would' (dự đoán quá khứ)",
            "review": "Future-in-the-past: would + V. Khi động từ tường thuật ở quá khứ (predicted, said, thought), 'will' lùi thành 'would'.",
            "example": "many critics predicted that it **would make** public libraries obsolete",
            "analysis": "'would make' là 'would + V nguyên thể' diễn tả một dự đoán về tương lai nhìn từ quá khứ (future-in-the-past). Nó nằm trong mệnh đề danh ngữ 'that…' làm tân ngữ cho 'predicted'. Đây là cách chuẩn để tường thuật một lời tiên đoán đã có trong quá khứ."
          },
