      "slug": "the-history-of-the-umbrella",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "It is something millions of people carry on rainy days, yet the umbrella was not **invent**ed\nto keep off the rain. Its story stretches back thousands of years and began under the sun,\nnot the clouds.\n\nThe earliest umbrellas were really parasols, used for **shade**. More than four thousand\nyears ago, people in places like ancient Egypt and China used them to stay out of the sun.\nNo one knows exactly who invented the first one. A parasol was often a sign of high\n**status**: a servant would hold one over an important person.\n\nThe Chinese made a big step forward. They were the first to make **waterproof** umbrellas\nby adding wax to the paper, so one could give **shelter** in the rain too. A **frame** of\nthin **rib**s held the cover open.\n\nFor years in Europe, umbrellas were seen as items for women. That changed thanks to an\nEnglish writer called Jonas Hanway, who carried an umbrella across England until it became\na **fashion** for men. Later, in 1852, Samuel Fox built a much stronger umbrella with steel\nribs.\n\nToday's umbrellas are **lightweight** and **foldable**, small enough to fit in a bag. From\na sign of power, the umbrella has become an **essential** everyday object that almost anyone\ncan own.",
