      "source_body_markdown": "In 1961, the meteorologist Edward Lorenz stumbled upon something that would quietly\nunsettle both **forecast**ing and philosophy. Rounding one number in his early computer\nweather model from six digits to three, he expected at most a tiny and harmless\ndiscrepancy; instead, the new run soon **diverged** entirely from the old, producing a\nwholly different imaginary climate. The atmosphere, it gradually turned out, was\n**deterministic** — governed throughout by fixed physical laws — yet at the same time\ngenuinely **chaotic**.\n\nThe defining signature of chaos is an extreme **sensitivity** to **initial** conditions.\nIn such a system, even the most minuscule errors in the opening measurements are\n**amplified** exponentially over time, until two near-identical forecasts eventually\ndrift violently apart. This is emphatically not a mere matter of slow or poorly\nprogrammed computers; even flawless instruments and infinite computing power would still\nrun up against a predictability **horizon**, a point beyond which any honest prediction\ncollapses into little more than guesswork.\n\nIf the physical systems studied by Lorenz were this stubborn, the human ones proved\neven more slippery still. In a remarkable body of work begun in the 1980s, the political\npsychologist Philip Tetlock patiently tracked many thousands of confident expert\npredictions about real-world political events. His startling central finding was that\nthe most assured television **pundit**s, the very people most often invited to comment,\ntended on average to forecast no better than blind chance.\n\nYet, crucially, not all forecasters turned out to be equal. Tetlock also identified a\nsmall minority — whom he memorably dubbed \"superforecasters\" — who consistently and\nmeasurably outperformed everyone else. They think in carefully **probabilistic** terms,\nattaching explicit odds to outcomes rather than issuing flat, all-or-nothing\npredictions; they constantly **calibrate** their confidence against their actual record;\nand only when fresh evidence arrives do they **revise** their standing estimates.\n\nWhat links these two very different stories together is a single, shared lesson in\n**humility**. The future is not some written cloth merely waiting to be unrolled and\nread out; for certain systems it is simply unknowable beyond a particular point. How far\nahead we can usefully see — itself wholly **contingent** on the kind of system and the\nkind of question we choose to ask — is perhaps the more useful question, far more so\nthan whether prediction is possible at all, a matter that, for now, remains very much\nopen.",
      "expected_metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Thấy 'Only when/Only if' đầu câu thì mệnh đề CHÍNH (vế sau) mới đảo trợ động từ — đừng đảo nhầm mệnh đề thời gian.",
            "point": "Đảo ngữ với 'Only when' (inversion sau cụm trạng ngữ phủ định/giới hạn)",
            "review": "Đảo ngữ: Only when/Only if/Only after + (mệnh đề) + trợ động từ + S + V (đảo ở mệnh đề chính). 'Not until' cũng theo quy tắc này.",
            "example": "**Only when fresh evidence arrives do they revise** their standing estimates.",
            "analysis": "Cụm trạng ngữ giới hạn 'Only when fresh evidence arrives' đứng đầu buộc ĐẢO trợ động từ ở mệnh đề CHÍNH: 'do they revise'. Lưu ý chỉ đảo mệnh đề chính, còn mệnh đề thời gian 'when fresh evidence arrives' giữ trật tự S–V bình thường. Đây là đảo ngữ THẬT SỰ (Only + … + aux + S + V)."
          },
