    {
      "passage_id": "f6d5a9da-1593-415f-8f33-1711f9ff9812",
      "slug": "the-puzzle-of-animal-migration",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "Each autumn, millions of monarch butterflies leave eastern North America and fly\nthousands of kilometres to warm forests in Mexico — a place none of them has ever\nseen. With no map and no parents to follow, they somehow reach the same few mountains\nevery year. How such tiny creatures **navigate** has long been one of science's most\nfascinating puzzles.\n\nPart of the answer is that they rely on several **cue**s at once. On clear days many\nmigrating species use the sun as a **compass**, adjusting their direction by its\nposition in the sky. But the sun is not always visible, so simple **instinct** cannot\nfully explain the **migration**.\n\nA second clue comes from the Earth itself. Studies show that both birds and monarchs\ncan sense the planet's **magnetic field**. Rather than the field's strength, they read\nits **inclination**, using that angle to **orient** themselves along a north–south\nline. This sense is thought to work through a special protein called a\n**cryptochrome** in the eye or antennae, which is **trigger**ed by light into a\nreaction sensitive to magnetism. Because the reaction depends on light, the animal can\nonly read its magnetic compass during the day, not in total darkness.\n\nFor the monarch, this magnetic **compass** appears to act as a **backup** system. The\nsun compass remains the main one, yet the magnetic compass helps the animal keep its\ndirection even on **overcast** days when the sun cannot be seen. It also guides the\ninsects as they **overwinter** far to the south, and cold mountain temperatures may\nhelp reset the compass on arrival. The whole journey is even more striking because no\nsingle butterfly completes the round trip; the return north is shared across several\nshort-lived generations. Much remains to be learned, but the journey is still a\n**remarkable** feat of nature.",
