      "slug": "the-return-of-the-tram",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "For much of the twentieth century, the **tram** was seen as old-fashioned. As cars\nfilled the cities after the 1950s, many authorities chose to **phase out** their\nrails, believing that buses and private vehicles were the future. Today, however,\nthat same elderly vehicle is enjoying a remarkable **comeback** across Europe.\n\nThe turning point came in the 1980s. As **congestion** and air pollution grew worse,\nFrench cities began to **revive** their lines; France alone now has close to 30\nnetworks, most of them rebuilt after once being shut down. In 1994 Strasbourg opened one of Europe's first\nfully **low-floor** systems, making it easy for **pedestrian**s and wheelchairs to\nboard. The first **route** ran for less than twelve kilometres, but it was quickly\nextended, and within a few decades it carried tens of millions of passengers each year.\nThe same pattern was repeated elsewhere, from Dublin to cities in Denmark and\nLuxembourg.\n\nThe appeal is easy to understand. Because trams run on electricity, they produce no\n**emissions** on the street, and a single tram has the **capacity** of several buses.\nBy moving large numbers of people along one **corridor**, a well-planned tram line can\ncut the number of cars in the centre and ease the daily rush hour. Many\ncities have also found that new lines help **regenerate** run-down areas as squares are\nreturned to people on foot. Shops and cafes often follow the rails, and quiet,\nneglected streets slowly come back to life.\n\nTrams are not a perfect solution. Laying the **infrastructure** is slow and expensive,\nand the lines cannot reach everywhere, so buses are still needed for many journeys.\nBuilding work can also disrupt traffic for months. Yet for the many European cities\nseeking cleaner, more **sustainable** transport, the humble tram appears to be an\nincreasingly attractive choice for the decade ahead.",
      "expected_metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
