      "slug": "why-we-procrastinate",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "source_body_markdown": "The night before an important essay is due, a student suddenly decides that the\nkitchen must be cleaned, old photographs sorted, and several videos watched. The\n**deadline** is hours away, yet the work itself remains untouched. This familiar\npattern is what psychologists call procrastination, and a widely cited meta-analysis\nby Canadian psychologist Piers Steel found that about\n20 per cent of adults are chronic procrastinators. The reason people\n**procrastinate** is rarely the one most of us assume.\n\nFor a long time, delaying important work was blamed on simple **laziness**. Yet\nresearchers now describe procrastination as an emotional problem rather than a\nproblem of weak willpower. When a task feels boring, confusing or threatening, it\ncan **trigger** unpleasant feelings, and the mind looks for a way to escape them.\nProcrastination, in this view, is mainly an attempt to **regulate** emotion, not\nto avoid effort.\n\nThe trouble is that the relief does not last. Faced with **anxiety** about a\ndifficult task, the brain reaches for **avoidance**, and switching to something\neasier brings a sense of **temporary** comfort. Because the human brain tends to\nvalue an immediate **reward** more highly than a distant one, the **impulse** to\nfeel better now usually defeats the wish to finish later. The task remains, the\nstress returns, and the cycle repeats.\n\nUnderstanding this emotional root suggests better ways to **cope with** delay.\nOne useful **strategy** is to break a large job into small parts and begin with a\nsingle, manageable step, since starting is often the hardest moment. Surprisingly,\n**self-compassion** also helps: people who forgive themselves for past delays tend\nto procrastinate less, while harsh self-criticism only adds to the anxiety that\ncaused the problem.\n\nNone of this makes procrastination easy to defeat, and no single method works for\neveryone. Still, the research points to a hopeful idea: by treating the feeling\nrather than scolding the will, many people may protect both their calm and their\n**productivity**.",
